# SYNOPSIS

```usage
renice -n num [-g | -p | -u] id ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`renice` is meant to add <num> to the nice value of each process <id>, clamped to the range −20 to 19, where a higher value asks for less of the processor.
On VectraOS it changes nothing (see BUGS).

: `-n` <num>
  The change, from −20 to 19.
: `-g`
  Each <id> is a process group, every process in it changed.
: `-p`
  Each <id> is a process: the default.
: `-u`
  Each <id> is a user's name, every process of that user changed.

`renice` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/renice`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/renice`.
There is no native `renice`.

# SEE ALSO

nice(1), sched(2), namespace(6); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/renice.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

Each <id> that fails is reported on standard error as `renice: getpriority` <id>`:`, `renice: setpriority` <id>`:` or `renice: getpwnam` <name>`:` and the error, and the next is tried.
A <num> or <id> out of range, a missing `-n` or no <id> prints a message or the usage message and exits 1.
The exit status is 1 if any <id> failed, otherwise 0.

# BUGS

`renice` always fails on VectraOS.
VectraOS's C library does not implement the `getpriority` and `setpriority` system calls, so each <id> is reported as `renice: getpriority` <id>`: Function not implemented`, and the library notes on the console that the system call is not implemented.
VectraOS has no nice values to change; its own scheduling calls, sched(2), are reserved and not yet implemented.

With `-u` there is no user to find: VectraOS has no `/etc/passwd`, so each name fails in `getpwnam` first.

The obsolescent form `renice` <priority> <id> ... is not accepted.
